A||||6||rodmcneill||Headliner sag?||||5||
Z||000000||rodmcneill||12-31-2000||09:24 PM||||I hear a lot about headliner sag. I have 2 GTs neither one is showing any signs of sag yet. Is it a matter of time? or is it climate effected or storage. Where does it normally sag? the edges or the middle?||||reg||5
Z||000001||ka4nkf||12-31-2000||10:14 PM||||I think the sag is caused from heat. if your car sits out in the sun then i think this is what causes it. the glue turns loose and then it sags. usually this happens in about 8 or 9 years. the sag will usually be in the middle. not too bad to replace.||||reg||1
Z||000002||FieroJoe||12-31-2000||10:56 PM||||Mine was pretty bad when I got the car (87).  I spent $100 for a place to redo it, and they got it done really fast and did a great job at it.  As long as the "matting" behind it is in good shape, then they should be able to do a good job.  The color match wasn't exact, but it still looks good.||||reg||1
Z||000003||skitime||12-31-2000||11:01 PM||||I just took my headliner to an upholsterer to redo on Thursday.  He looked in the factory sample book and even the 88 factory samples that never saw sun and heat had deteriorated and separated.  I decided not to use the orginal upholstery because of that.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Hank is Here||12-31-2000||11:26 PM||||My luck has been 13 years for GM headliners. From what I understand though it is not the glue that lets go but actually the foam detiorating<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||pred1tor83||12-31-2000||11:34 PM||||hank has it right the foam on the head liner deteirorates (SP) i had it happen to my 87 IROC Z-28||||reg||2
Z||000006||Mark||12-31-2000||11:38 PM||||Pretty much all "newer" cars that use the foam-backed nylon headliner material will eventually have the sagging problem.  It is due to deterioration of the foam backing.  It happened on my low mileage GT and also my low mileage VW GTI. I guess it's a heat/time/humidity thing. Fortunately, the fix isn't too expensive, even if done professionally.  If your handy, you can even do it yourself for about $30 in material and glue.  The headliner fabric is available at Joanne Fabrics, and the spray adhesive is available at NAPA. Someone even claimed they found the adhesive at Builders Square.  <P>Do a search on this subject for more info... there have been alot of posts. ||||reg||1
